{%- comment -%}
  The collection-page offer rail — up to five swipeable cards above the
  browse controls, saying what this store takes off a bag before the shopper
  has put anything in one.

  THE ORDER IS THE DESIGN
  Cards run by threshold, ascending. Five cards on a 390px screen means most
  shoppers see the first two and nothing else, so the order decides what the
  rail actually communicates — ordering by SIZE would put "₹700 over ₹5,000"
  in position two, which speaks to almost nobody at an average order near
  ₹1,668. Ascending threshold turns the swipe itself into the ladder: each
  card right is a bigger reward, further away.

  THREE VISUAL FAMILIES, BECAUSE THERE ARE THREE KINDS OF OFFER
    1  Buy 2 Get 1  — full brand crimson, wider. The only card with no
                      threshold at all, and the only one given the brand
                      colour, so it cannot be mistaken for a spend tier.
    2-4 the tiers   — one warm-neutral treatment deepening as the threshold
                      rises. They read as a set because they ARE a set: one
                      staircase, three rungs.
    5  prepaid      — green, deliberately unlike the rest. Not about how much
                      you spend; about how you pay.

  IT STATES, IT DOES NOT CLAIM
  Nothing here is marked "applied". A collection page has no cart total to
  reason from, and two of these five could not be resolved from one anyway:
  Buy 2 Get 1 turns on an item count, and the prepaid ₹100 turns on a payment
  method chosen at checkout. A rail that lit cards up would be making a
  promise it has no way to keep, so this one only ever lists what is on offer.

  WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM
  The three tiers read settings.reward_tier* — the same single source of truth
  as the cart ladder, the PDP reward bar and the sticky buy bar, so the rail
  cannot drift from them. Buy 2 Get 1 hangs off onrepeat-b2g1-on, which is the
  one answer all four B2G1 surfaces share. All of it is DISPLAY ONLY: the
  theme grants nothing, and every figure here must match the automatic
  discounts in Admin → Discounts.

  THE PREPAID CARD IS NOT A SHOPIFY DISCOUNT
  It is applied by Razorpay Magic Checkout, which is why it has its own
  setting rather than a reward tier, and why it is suppressed for USD
  visitors: they go to Shopify's native checkout (see onrepeat-usd-mode), where
  Razorpay never runs and the ₹100 would never arrive. Set the amount to 0 to
  drop the card. It must move together with the prepaid banner in the cart and
  cart-drawer sections, or the two surfaces disagree.

  ONLY WHERE THERE ARE GOODS
  An empty collection gets no rail: a spend ladder above "no products here"
  is advertising a bag the shopper cannot fill.

  This was briefly a five-product floor, on the theory that the rail would
  otherwise stand taller than the goods. It does not — four products is two
  rows of full-height product cards against 88px of rail — and the floor's
  only real effect was to hide the offers on /collections/shirts.

  Accepts:
  - collection: {Object} the collection being browsed. Required; the rail does
      not render without one.

  Usage (already wired):
    {%- render 'onrepeat-offer-rail', collection: collection -%}   ← sections/main-collection.liquid
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign or_show = false
  if settings.offer_rail_enabled and collection != blank and collection.all_products_count > 0
    assign or_show = true
  endif
-%}

{%- if or_show -%}
  {%- liquid
    capture or_b2g1_raw
      render 'onrepeat-b2g1-on'
    endcapture
    assign or_b2g1 = or_b2g1_raw | strip

    comment
      `strip` is not optional here, exactly as in onrepeat-usd-mode's own docs — a
      stray newline would make the comparison false and silently send a US
      shopper the prepaid card they can never redeem.
    endcomment
    capture or_usd_raw
      render 'onrepeat-usd-mode'
    endcapture
    assign or_usd = or_usd_raw | strip

    assign or_pct = settings.reward_percent | default: 5
    assign or_t1 = settings.reward_tier1_amount | default: 1499
    assign or_t2 = settings.reward_tier2_amount | default: 3000
    assign or_t3 = settings.reward_tier3_amount | default: 5000
    assign or_f2 = settings.reward_tier2_flat | default: 300
    assign or_f3 = settings.reward_tier3_flat | default: 700

    comment
      Liquid's `default` replaces nil/false/empty, never 0, so a merchant who
      types 0 to retire the prepaid card keeps their 0.
    endcomment
    assign or_prepaid = settings.offer_rail_prepaid_off | default: 100
    if or_usd == 'true'
      assign or_prepaid = 0
    endif

    comment
      Money, the same way the cart ladder does it.

      The flat amounts go through money-display 'no_zeros', whose two branches
      are both already right: dollars for a US visitor, and
      money_without_trailing_zeros for everyone else — "₹300", not "₹300.00",
      which is what plain `money` would print on this shop.

      The spend thresholds cannot use one style for both. In USD they must
      round UP ('ceil') so a displayed target never arrives sooner than the
      real unlock; in INR they are exact figures the merchant typed and must
      be echoed as typed. So they branch.
    endcomment
    assign or_t1_paise = or_t1 | times: 100
    assign or_t2_paise = or_t2 | times: 100
    assign or_t3_paise = or_t3 | times: 100
    assign or_f2_paise = or_f2 | times: 100
    assign or_f3_paise = or_f3 | times: 100
    assign or_pre_paise = or_prepaid | times: 100

    if or_usd == 'true'
      capture or_t1_spend
        render 'money-display', amount: or_t1_paise, style: 'ceil'
      endcapture
      capture or_t2_spend
        render 'money-display', amount: or_t2_paise, style: 'ceil'
      endcapture
      capture or_t3_spend
        render 'money-display', amount: or_t3_paise, style: 'ceil'
      endcapture
    else
      assign or_t1_spend = or_t1_paise | money_without_trailing_zeros
      assign or_t2_spend = or_t2_paise | money_without_trailing_zeros
      assign or_t3_spend = or_t3_paise | money_without_trailing_zeros
    endif

    capture or_f2_txt
      render 'money-display', amount: or_f2_paise, style: 'no_zeros'
    endcapture
    capture or_f3_txt
      render 'money-display', amount: or_f3_paise, style: 'no_zeros'
    endcapture
    capture or_pre_txt
      render 'money-display', amount: or_pre_paise, style: 'no_zeros'
    endcapture
  -%}

  <div class="onrepeat-offers" role="region" aria-labelledby="OnRepeatOffersHead">
    <p class="onrepeat-offers__head" id="OnRepeatOffersHead">Exclusive offers</p>

    <ul class="onrepeat-offers__track" role="list">
      {%- if or_b2g1 != blank -%}
        <li class="onrepeat-offers__card onrepeat-offers__card--hero">
          <b class="onrepeat-offers__v">Buy 2, get <em>1 free</em></b>
          <span class="onrepeat-offers__c">Add any three pieces</span>
        </li>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if or_t1 > 0 -%}
        <li class="onrepeat-offers__card onrepeat-offers__card--t1">
          <b class="onrepeat-offers__v"><em>{{ or_pct }}%</em> off</b>
          <span class="onrepeat-offers__c">Over {{ or_t1_spend | strip }}</span>
        </li>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if or_t2 > 0 and or_f2 > 0 -%}
        <li class="onrepeat-offers__card onrepeat-offers__card--t2">
          <b class="onrepeat-offers__v"><em>{{ or_f2_txt | strip }}</em> off</b>
          <span class="onrepeat-offers__c">Over {{ or_t2_spend | strip }}</span>
        </li>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if or_t3 > 0 and or_f3 > 0 -%}
        <li class="onrepeat-offers__card onrepeat-offers__card--t3">
          <b class="onrepeat-offers__v"><em>{{ or_f3_txt | strip }}</em> off</b>
          <span class="onrepeat-offers__c">Over {{ or_t3_spend | strip }}</span>
        </li>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if or_prepaid > 0 -%}
        <li class="onrepeat-offers__card onrepeat-offers__card--pay">
          <b class="onrepeat-offers__v"><em>{{ or_pre_txt | strip }}</em> off</b>
          <span class="onrepeat-offers__c">Paying online at checkout</span>
        </li>
      {%- endif -%}
    </ul>

    {%- comment -%}
      One honest statement for the whole rail rather than "AUTO" stamped on
      every card. Every offer above really is automatic — the tiers and the
      free piece are automatic discounts in Admin, and the prepaid ₹100 is
      applied by Razorpay at checkout with no code to enter (the cart drawer's
      prepaid_checkout_code is empty for the same reason).

      The dots are built by script and stay absent without it: they say how
      many cards there are and where you are, which is information the rail
      can live without, not markup that has to be there before it is true.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="onrepeat-offers__foot">
      <span class="onrepeat-offers__auto">No codes — all applied automatically</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    (function () {
      'use strict';
      if (window.__onrepeatOfferRail) return;
      window.__onrepeatOfferRail = true;

      function wire(root) {
        var track = root.querySelector('.onrepeat-offers__track');
        var foot = root.querySelector('.onrepeat-offers__foot');
        if (!track || !foot) return;

        var cards = track.querySelectorAll('.onrepeat-offers__card');
        if (cards.length < 2) return;

        var dots = document.createElement('div');
        dots.className = 'onrepeat-offers__dots';
        dots.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
        for (var i = 0; i < cards.length; i++) dots.appendChild(document.createElement('i'));
        var pips = dots.querySelectorAll('i');
        pips[0].className = 'is-on';
        foot.appendChild(dots);

        /* On a wide screen all five cards are already visible, and a position
           indicator for a row nobody has to scroll is noise. */
        function fit() {
          dots.hidden = track.scrollWidth <= track.clientWidth + 1;
        }

        track.addEventListener('scroll', function () {
          var max = track.scrollWidth - track.clientWidth;
          var n = max > 0 ? Math.round((track.scrollLeft / max) * (pips.length - 1)) : 0;
          for (var j = 0; j < pips.length; j++) pips[j].className = j === n ? 'is-on' : '';
        }, { passive: true });

        window.addEventListener('resize', fit, { passive: true });
        fit();
      }

      function init() {
        var rails = document.querySelectorAll('.onrepeat-offers');
        for (var i = 0; i < rails.length; i++) wire(rails[i]);
      }

      if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
        document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', init);
      } else {
        init();
      }
    })();
  </script>
{%- endif -%}
